import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Retro: handler-interpreted atoms — ADR 0007 layer 2 lands","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/handler-proposals.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/handler-proposals.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/handler-proposals.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="retro-handler-interpreted-atoms-—-adr-0007-layer-2-lands" tabindex="-1">Retro: handler-interpreted atoms — ADR 0007 layer 2 lands <a class="header-anchor" href="#retro-handler-interpreted-atoms-—-adr-0007-layer-2-lands" aria-label="Permalink to “Retro: handler-interpreted atoms — ADR 0007 layer 2 lands”">​</a></h1><p>Date: 2026-10-15 Context: ADR 0007 layer 2 (&quot;handler-interpreted atoms — semantics by denotation&quot;). <code>genkernel</code> mints handlers whose kernel evaluates the spec&#39;s own spelled body — the elementwise special case. This lands the generalisation the ADR asked for: a <strong>structured handler grammar</strong> whose candidate bodies are proposed for a measured subterm and verified numerically against the pre-mint term on the measured domain — the same bar as a law. Files: <code>packages/mlopt-discovery/src/mlopt_discovery/handler_synth.py</code> (the whole mechanism), <code>tests/test_discovery_handler_synth.py</code> (37 tests, 100% module coverage). Nothing else touched; core, carriers and the sink are unchanged.</p><h2 id="the-grammar-descriptors-are-plain-data" tabindex="-1">The grammar (descriptors are plain data) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-grammar-descriptors-are-plain-data" aria-label="Permalink to “The grammar (descriptors are plain data)”">​</a></h2><p>Five families, all evaluated by <code>eval_body</code> — no eval/exec, a structured walk:</p><ul><li><code>(&quot;ew&quot;, sub)</code> — elementwise replay; the <code>genkernel</code> tier re-spelled. Proposed only for genuinely elementwise specs.</li><li><code>(&quot;contract&quot;, eq, lhs, rhs)</code> — <code>einsum(eq, EW(lhs), EW(rhs))</code>; the two factors are elementwise trees over metavar subsets that cover the spec&#39;s metas. <code>eq</code> ∈ 7 closed equations (the four batched-matmul orientations, transposed output, matvec, inner product).</li><li><code>(&quot;fold&quot;, a, b, state, axis)</code> — <code>acc_t = ew(a)_t ⊙ acc_{t-1} + ew(b)_t</code> walked along <code>axis</code>, operands broadcast into the step space (the scan tier&#39;s <code>_seq_args</code> convention).</li><li><code>(&quot;fold_emit&quot;, a, b, state, emit, axis)</code> — the recurrence plus a per-step projection <code>out_t = ew(emit)</code> where <code>emit</code> may read the running accumulator through the reserved <code>&quot;$acc&quot;</code> leaf; outputs stack back on <code>axis</code>.</li><li><code>(&quot;fold_zip&quot;, steps, state)</code> — the flat chronological fold: <code>acc = a_i ⊙ acc + b_i</code> over a tuple of metavar pairs — the semantics an unrolled <code>applyd</code> nest already spells.</li></ul><p>Elementwise factors come from a bounded expression grammar (<code>_ew_exprs</code>): 7 unary + 4 binary ops, ≤ 2 op nodes, spec constants admitted as atoms. Proposal enumeration is deterministic, simplest-first (total op count), round-robin across columns (partitions / states / pairings) so no column&#39;s deep region starves another&#39;s cheap candidates.</p><h2 id="verify-or-refuse-measured" tabindex="-1">Verify-or-refuse, measured <a class="header-anchor" href="#verify-or-refuse-measured" aria-label="Permalink to “Verify-or-refuse, measured”">​</a></h2><p><code>verify_body</code> draws fp64 operands per domain entry (paired draws — same seed for every candidate) plus an adversarial re-draw under an offset seed; the gate is <code>allclose(rtol=1e-9, atol=1e-12)</code> against the spelled spec evaluated through the ambient bindings. <code>mint_handlers</code> returns <code>(handlers, report)</code> — every item gets a verdict row: minted, covered, or refused (with the measured best-seen error and which of three refusals applied: outside the grammar, grammar exhausted, budget cap).</p><p>The measured table (seed 0, fp64, one domain binding per spec):</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>site spec</th><th>verdict</th><th>family</th><th>tried</th><th>max_err</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>matmul(mul(S,D),V)</code> — the <code>om_decayed</code> element</td><td>minted</td><td>contract</td><td>13</td><td>8.9e-16</td></tr><tr><td><code>matmul(mul(mul(S,D),M),V)</code> — masked inner-chunk</td><td>minted</td><td>contract</td><td>31</td><td>1.8e-15</td></tr><tr><td>same, batched <code>(B,T,K)</code> operands</td><td>minted</td><td>contract</td><td>13</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>closed-form <code>H·∏a + Σ b·(∏a/∏≤a)</code></td><td>minted</td><td>fold</td><td>10</td><td>4.4e-16</td></tr><tr><td><code>Q ⊙ (running acc)</code> sequence</td><td>minted</td><td>fold_emit</td><td>417</td><td>1.3e-15</td></tr><tr><td><code>applyd(aff_diag(A,B), applyd(aff_diag(C,D),H))</code></td><td>minted</td><td>fold_zip</td><td>292</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td><code>sum(X, dim=-1)</code></td><td>refused</td><td>—</td><td>0</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>exp(matmul(X,Y))</code></td><td>refused</td><td>—</td><td>1200</td><td>best 1.82</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The pay the task named is real: <code>om_decayed</code>&#39;s <code>(s⊙d)@v</code> — a pattern that today needs a hand-written executor row (<code>om.TORCH_BINDINGS[&quot;om_decayed&quot;]</code>) — mints as <code>(&quot;contract&quot;, &quot;...ik,...kj-&gt;...ij&quot;, (&quot;mul&quot;,&quot;D&quot;,&quot;S&quot;), &quot;V&quot;)</code>, verified fp64 against the spelled term, and the minted op lowers through <code>minted_sink</code> end to end. Same for the masked inner-chunk form <code>((s·d)·m)@v</code>, the chunked fold&#39;s closed form (<code>fold</code>), a <code>q ⊙ acc</code> projection sequence (<code>fold_emit</code>), and the two-step unrolled <code>applyd</code> nest (<code>fold_zip</code> — bitwise 0.0). The one-step <code>fold_zip</code> also mints the carrier&#39;s base step <code>a⊙h+b</code> verbatim.</p><h2 id="the-falsification-read-—-adr-0007-3-handler-grammar-too-weak" tabindex="-1">The falsification read — ADR 0007 #3 (&quot;handler grammar too weak&quot;) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-falsification-read-—-adr-0007-3-handler-grammar-too-weak" aria-label="Permalink to “The falsification read — ADR 0007 #3 (&quot;handler grammar too weak&quot;)”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Not falsified.</strong> Every one of the three non-elementwise template shapes the ADR names — contraction, fold, fold+project — produced a verified handler against its pre-mint subterm inside a ≤ ~420-candidate bounded search. The grammar is strong enough to write a scan: <code>(&quot;fold&quot;, &quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;, &quot;H&quot;, -2)</code> IS the sequential recurrence, verified against the spelled closed form.</p><p>Honest boundaries, measured not guessed:</p><ul><li><strong>The seq-fold needs a spelled fold.</strong> <code>(&quot;fold&quot;,…)</code> cannot verify against <code>applyd(aff_diag(A,B),H)</code> — the ambient <code>applyd</code> is one map application <code>a⊙h+b</code>, a different function of the same bound values. Verification only exists where the pre-mint term itself spells the fold (the closed form, or the unrolled nest <code>fold_zip</code> covers). A site whose intended reading is the fold of sequence operands is a Layer-3 question — the atom names a semantics nothing spelled — not a Layer-2 verification failure.</li><li><strong>Reductions/softmax are outside the grammar</strong> — <code>tried: 0</code> refusals. Named template shapes missing today: reduction axes (<code>sum</code>/<code>mean</code>/<code>prod</code> as first-class templates), row-normalised contractions (softmax&#39;s <code>exp/sum</code> shape), and contractions over more than two factors.</li><li><strong>Emit-only operands required a coverage fix</strong> — the first enumeration forced every non-state meta into <code>a</code>/<code>b</code>, so <code>q ⊙ acc</code> sites (where <code>q</code> feeds only the projection) were unreachable. The shipped enumeration covers leftovers by the emit; a meta may serve both roles.</li><li><strong>Budget is the honest cap</strong> — <code>exp(matmul)</code> burned all 1200 contract candidates (best err 1.82). Deep folds in <code>fold_emit</code>-shaped space cost ~400 tries; anything deeper is a budgeted gap, not an unseen one.</li><li><strong>Spec constants only.</strong> The atom pool is the spec&#39;s own constant leaves plus metas; fresh constants (e.g. a <code>0.5</code> a handler would need) are unproposable — a named gap.</li><li><strong>Emit metas can&#39;t mix freely.</strong> An emit expression draws from rest ∪ <code>{$acc}</code> ∪ consts; deeper emit trees (two ops inside the projection) are bounded out.</li></ul><h2 id="delivery-provenance" tabindex="-1">Delivery + provenance <a class="header-anchor" href="#delivery-provenance" aria-label="Permalink to “Delivery + provenance”">​</a></h2><p>A minted entry is a handler-table row <code>{pattern, kernel, args, handler_body, provenance}</code> — the same shape <code>genkernel</code>&#39;s tiers mint, so <code>claim</code>/<code>handle</code> binds it and <code>handlers_fragment</code> wraps it as cells with no new machinery. <code>minted_bindings</code> interprets the descriptor (verified semantics, not a replay); <code>minted_sink</code> extends <code>OpTable.core()</code> exactly like <code>gen_sink</code>. <code>handler_record</code> /<code>handler_from_record</code> JSON-round-trips the atom — grammar path, verified domain, seed, tried count, max_err — the minted atom is provenance data, not live code.</p>`,17)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};